Nepal has expanded security restrictions to a fourth district after fresh protests linked to recent communal violence prompted authorities to impose an indefinite curfew in parts of Saptari district in Madhesh province. The latest restrictions come as curfews and prohibitory orders remain in force in parts of Sunsari, Dhanusha and Siraha, where unrest has left at least three people dead.

According to Nepal Police, eight people have been arrested in Madhesh province for allegedly inciting communal violence, including five suspects from Siraha and three from Dhanusha.

The District Administration Office in Saptari enforced an indefinite curfew in parts of Rajbiraj municipality from 8 am on Friday after demonstrations were held in response to the violence in neighbouring districts. Security has also been intensified across Nepal's southern Terai belt along the Indian border to prevent the unrest from spreading.

The violence first erupted on Sunday in Sunsari district of Koshi province, where members of two religious communities holding separate events clashed over the use of loudspeakers and the display of religious flags. Police said the confrontation escalated after a verbal dispute turned violent. One person was killed and more than a dozen others, including police personnel, were injured when security forces opened fire to disperse the crowd.

A mosque was set on fire during a protest in Nepal. Authorities have not yet released a full account of the incident. The attack deserves unequivocal condemnation and would represent a serious act of communal violence and is likely to have serious impact.#Nepal#Siraha… pic.twitter.com/VrPcq3436T var taboolaIntMid = false; window.addEventListener('scroll', () => { let pageYOffsetbottom = window.pageYOffset; if (pageYOffsetbottom > 300 && !taboolaIntMid && showAdsToUser()) { window._taboola = window._taboola || []; if (typeof showAdsToUser !== "function" || showAdsToUser()) { _taboola.push({ mode: 'thumbnails-mid-article', container: 'taboola-mid-article-thumbnails', placement: 'Mid Article Thumbnails', target_type: 'mix' }); taboolaIntMid = true; commonGAeventTaboolaPV('Mid Article Thumbnails'); } } }); — Sunanda Acharya (@Sunanda_Iam) July 31, 2026

The unrest later spread into Madhesh province. Authorities said a teenager was killed during clashes in Siraha district on Thursday, while another person who had been injured in the earlier violence in Sunsari also succumbed to injuries. In a separate incident the same night, police constable Narad Pariyar was shot by an unidentified attacker in Lahan municipality of Siraha. He sustained a gunshot wound to his right thigh and is undergoing treatment at a hospital in Biratnagar.

President Ram Chandra Paudel and Prime Minister Balendra Shah appealed for peace and restraint amid the escalating tensions. In a statement issued Thursday, Paudel urged political parties, civil society groups and citizens to uphold social harmony and national unity.

Shah later addressed the nation, assuring that the government would conduct a neutral and transparent investigation into the violence and ensure accountability for those responsible.

The prime minister also held consultations on Friday with senior Madhesh-based political leaders, including Mahantha Thakur, Rajendra Mahato, C K Raut, Prabhu Shah and Samim Ansari, to discuss the evolving situation. He separately met Members of Parliament from the ruling Rastriya Swatantra Party representing Madhesh, with discussions focusing on development work and the concerns of Madhesi communities.

Meanwhile, the District Security Committee in the border district of Sarlahi has made it mandatory for people entering Nepal from across the border to carry valid identity documents as authorities tighten surveillance in sensitive areas.

