President Ram Chandra Paudel also convened consultations with former President Ram Baran Yadav and former Vice President Paramananda Jha to discuss the deteriorating situation. Participants stressed the need for collective efforts involving political parties, civil society and religious leaders to preserve Nepal’s long-standing tradition of social and religious harmony.

According to reports, curfews and prohibitory orders are now in force across parts of Sunsari, Siraha, Dhanusha and Saptari districts. Police have arrested eight people on allegations of inciting communal violence, while security has been tightened across Nepal’s southern Terai belt bordering India.

The violence has also renewed debate over the role of misinformation and social media in fuelling communal tensions. Analysts cited in local reports said misleading photographs, recycled videos and false claims circulated rapidly online, spreading fear and anger far beyond the original site of the clash before authorities could issue clarifications.

Several commentators have also linked the recurring communal unrest in Nepal’s southern plains to long-standing local grievances, weak state responses, and the growing influence of organised religious mobilisation in border districts. They argue that disputes over processions, loudspeakers and religious symbols increasingly become flashpoints in a region where communities have traditionally coexisted.

Nepal, where Hindus constitute about 80 per cent of the population and Muslims account for just over five per cent, has witnessed several major episodes of communal violence over the past two decades. Muslims make up about 13 per cent of the population in Madhesh Province and around 7 per cent in Lumbini Province, both higher than Nepal’s national average. Most of the recent communal clashes have occurred in Terai districts such as Sunsari, Dhanusha, Rautahat, Parsa and Sarlahi, where Muslim populations are relatively larger.

